[[!comment format=mdwn
 username="joey"
 subject="""comment 6"""
 date="2026-10-12T16:17:16Z"
 content="""
Exfiltration from an encrypted special remote needs the attacker to know
the encryption cipher.

If I have an encrypted special remote, and I opt to publish the git
repository, the attacker *does* have enough information for this attack.

They can reuse the cipher field of the config of my special remote in
their special remote. With encryption=shared, that's not encrypted and it
obviously works. With encryption=pubkey or encryption=hybrid, it is
encrypted, but my gpg key will decrypt it and be able to access files on my
encrypted special remote.

If I don't publish my git repository, my encrypted special remotes are safe
from this attack.
"""]]
